WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.410
There was a time when it felt like All-American
Hollywood dreamboat Chris O'Donnell was everywhere,

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.250
having made a name for himself in the '90s
in hit movies like Fried Green Tomatoes and

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.360
Scent of a Woman.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.540
Since 2009, the actor has appeared as G. Callen
in the CBS drama NCIS: Los Angeles.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.500
But blockbuster roles appear to be in short
supply.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.540
So why won't Hollywood cast Chris O'Donnell
anymore?

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.380
Batman &amp; Robin sucked

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.530
For many actors, landing a role in a superhero
movie is a major career boost, which appeared

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.310
to be the case when O'Donnell took his first
turn as the Caped Crusader's sidekick in the

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.950
bad, but bearable, Batman Forever.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.240
Unfortunately, the film's follow-up, Batman
&amp; Robin, was an infamous dud that put the

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.810
Batman franchise on ice.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.150
"Stay cool, bird boy."

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:49.650
The film put an end to the quartet of films,
and Batman's next live-action outing didn't

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:54.649
come until Christian Bale's raspy Dark Detective
appeared nearly a decade later.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:57.790
Plans had been in the works for a fifth film
that would have centered on Robin, and the

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:01.320
solo hero film might have been a career breakthrough
for O'Donnell.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.020
But Batman &amp; Robin was such a colossal failure
that further plans for the series were scrapped.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.000
Instead of establishing himself as a box office
powerhouse, O'Donnell went down with the ship.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.999
He rejected Men In Black

00:01:13.999 --> 00:01:18.479
In another, weirder timeline, audiences are
getting jiggy with Chris O'Donnell instead

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:23.390
of Will Smith as the lead in the hugely popular
1997 hit, Men in Black.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.969
But in our reality, the former Robin said
"no thank you" to a chance at the recurring

00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:28.969
role.

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:33.259
Instead, O'Donnell virtually disappeared in
the years following the movie's release...

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.950
perhaps having left Hollywood in search of
a neuralyzer to erase his memories of passing

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.029
on the role of Agent J, who's infinitely cooler
than Robin anyhow.

00:01:41.029 --> 00:01:43.149
"Holy corpuscles!"

00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:45.229
His comeback also tanked

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:49.101
Following a couple of years away from the
industry, O'Donnell returned to action in

00:01:49.101 --> 00:01:53.299
movies you've probably never heard of, like
Cookie's Fortune, The Bachelor, and Vertical

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:54.299
Limit.

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:57.529
None of them made much of an impact, and Hollywood
executives may have viewed their relative

00:01:57.529 --> 00:02:01.770
failure as an indication that O'Donnell's
commercial appeal was damaged beyond repair

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.650
by those terrible old Bat-nipples.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:07.090
The jump to TV

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.230
With feature opportunities drying up and a
growing family, O'Donnell turned to small

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.010
arcs on shows like Grey's Anatomy and Two
and a Half Men, which kept him out of the

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.280
spotlight while allowing him to continue working
consistently as an actor.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.739
O'Donnell finally found a home on the small
screen with a starring role as the head crime-fighting

00:02:23.739 --> 00:02:26.140
orphan on NCIS: Los Angeles.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.470
So maybe he did get that third shot at Robin
after all.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.470
Family first

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.730
Ultimately, the choice to shy away from mega-fame
was O'Donnell's.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:39.520
The actor had been married to his wife Caroline
Fentress since 1997, and the couple has five

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.069
children together.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:44.840
O'Donnell said that he intentionally backed
away from fame so he could focus on his role

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.360
as a father and husband.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.930
What's next?

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.510
O'Donnell's career may not be what it was
during the '90s, but that doesn't mean the

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.090
door has closed.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.730
He's proven he has the chops to act in everything
from rom-coms to action films, and he's building

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:03.019
a loyal fanbase among NCIS: Los Angeles viewers
since 2009.

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:06.590
It's something that could translate to box
office gold, given the right role.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.609
Until then, however, he seems content to earn
a steady living while loving the life he's

00:03:10.609 --> 00:03:12.489
carved out for himself.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:14.400
And we forgive you for Batman &amp; Robin.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:23.180
"I hate to disappoint you but...rubber lips
are immune to your charms."

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.409
Well, we almost forgive you.

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:26.409
Thanks for watching!

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:28.760
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.890
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

